# Expectation record for 09_optimization_levels/003_volatile_side_effect_preservation.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 003_volatile_side_effect_preservation
area               = 09_optimization_levels
description        = Volatile side-effect sequence preserved without elision, reordering or coalescing at -O0, -O1 and -O2
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Free of undefined and unspecified behaviour. This is the only program in the area that
modifies volatile objects, and every modification is a separate full statement with its own
sequence point, so no ordering question arises: the sequence of observable side effects is
fully determined by the order of the statements. The compound form g_counter = g_counter + 1
is well defined because C11 6.5.16p3 sequences the update of the left operand after the
value computations of both operands, so the read and the write within one assignment are
ordered. No object is modified twice between sequence points. No signed overflow: the
counter reaches only 8 and the sink holds at most 33. There is no shift, no division, no
pointer arithmetic, no aliasing violation, and no read of uninitialized storage, because
step1, step2, step3, loop_total and i are all assigned before use. No printf argument
expression has a side effect; every argument is a plain read. No dependence on padding
bytes or on relative addresses.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
No implementation-defined behaviour is observed. Widths are normalized: only int is used,
which is 32 bits on all four targets, and long is never used because sizeof(long) is 4 on
i686 and 8 on the other three targets. Plain char is not used, so the measured plain-char
signedness difference (signed on x86-64 and i686, unsigned on AArch64 and RISC-V 64) cannot
be observed. No address, pointer value, sizeof or _Alignof result is printed. No floating
point is used. The volatile qualification of g_counter and g_sink is required semantics, not
an implementation-defined property: every access must be materialized on every target at
every optimization level, so the printed sequence is identical everywhere.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
counter_initial=0
after_step1 returned=1 counter=1
after_step2 returned=2 counter=2
after_step3 returned=3 counter=3
sink_a=11
sink_b=22
sink_c=33
loop_step=0 counter=4
loop_step=1 counter=5
loop_step=2 counter=6
loop_step=3 counter=7
loop_step=4 counter=8
counter_final=8
END
